title:: Object Prototyping
categories:: Guides
summary:: Writing runtime 'classes'
related:: Classes/IdentityDictionary

You cannot create nor edit a class without recompiling the language... but what if you need that?

Object prototyping is a middle ground that lets you build class–like structures at runtime.



It works by taking advantage of the class link::Classes/ProtoObject::, which can be created through the shortcut syntax code::#( ... )::.

note::In previous versions of Supercollider, this was implemented use link::Classes/Event::, there were many limitations and subtle issues with this approach and it is recommend to switch to the new approach.::

section:: Instance Variables (Members)

Instance variable–like things can be created by just adding keys to an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary::

code::
a = #(meow: 10)
a.meow // 10
a.meow = 20;
a.meow // 20;
::

Unlike normal classes, you can create new instance variables at runtime.

code::
a = #(meow: 10)
a.woof = 4;
a.woof // 4
a.meow // 10
::


Although this may be disabled by setting code::protoObjectLock:: to code::true::

code::
a = #(meow: 10, protoObjectLock: true)
a.woof = 10; // Prints error
::

section:: Methods

By assigning a function to a key, you can call that function as if it were a normal instance method.

The first argument passed to the function is the proto object itself.
Usually, we call this argument code::self::.

code::
a = #( 
	word: "meow",
	speak: { |self, adverb|
		"I say '%' %.".format(self.word, adverb) 
	},
);
a.speak(adverb: "loudly") // I say 'meow' loudly.
a.word = "woof";
a.speak(adverb: "softly") // I say 'woof' softly.
::

link::Reference/Functions#Variable Arguments:: also work.

code::
a = #( 
	speak: {|self... words, kwargs| "I say '%' %.".format(words, kwargs.asDict[\adverb]) }
);

a.speak("meow", "woof", adverb: "loudly") // I say "[meow, woof]" loudly.
::

section:: Constructors

There is no direct way to mimic a constructor, but this isn't a problem, as we can simply make a function that returns the link::Classes/IdentityDictionary::.

code::
(
var makeSpeaker = { |word|
	#(
		word: word,
		speak: { |self, adverb| "I say \"%\" %.".format(self.word, adverb) }
	)
};

var cat = makeSpeaker.("meow");
var dog = makeSpeaker.("woof");

cat.speak("loudly").postln; // I say "meow" loudly.
dog.speak("quietly").postln; // I say "woof" quietly.
)
::

section:: Private Instance Variables (Members)

These can be implemented inside the constructor function as normal variables, however, you don't add them to the proto object.

code::
(
var makeSpeaker = {
	var secretWords = "I'm a cat";
	#(
		assign_secret: { |self, secret| secretWords = secret },
		speak_secrets: { |self| "My secret is \"%\".".format(secretWords.value) }
	)
};

var p1 = makeSpeaker.();

p1.speak_secrets().postln; // My secret is "I'm a cat".
p1.assign_secret("I'm a dog");
p1.speak_secrets().postln; // My secret is "I'm a dog".
)
::

section:: Inheritance 

Inheritance is done by setting the code::super:: key to another ProtoObject. 
You cannot use another type of dictionary.
You are responsible for not creating infinite loops, it will freeze supercollider!

code::
p = #(speak: {|self| "I say %".format(self.word) });
b = #(super: p, word: \meow);

b.speak // I say meow
::

It is possible to create long chains of inheritance.

When you call a message on a proto object, it will use the first implementation it finds. 

section:: Interesting things that can be done.

Since the creation of link::Classes/AbstractObject:: and link::Classes/ProtoObject:: supercollider supports polymorphism to a much higher level with object prototyping.
This means you can do things previously impossible, and potentially dangerous/fun!

Essentially, you can now override every single message in supercollider, except code::identityHash::, code::==::, and code::!==::, which together define the concept of identity, 
and some compiler 'inlined' control flow  messages such as code::if::, code::and::, code::or::, code::!?::, code::??::, code::switch::. 

However you can override a lot of basic supercollider functionality, which can be very powerful and surprising, be careful!

Below is a list of some of the fun things you can do.

subsection:: doesNotUnderstand

code::
a = #(
	doesNotUnderstand: { |self, selector ...args, kwargs|
		"I'm sorry Dave, I'm afraid I can't do that."
	}
);
a.shutdown //  "I'm sorry Dave, I'm afraid I can't do that."
::


code::doesNotUnderstand:: is a very important method that is called whenever an object doesn't understand the message, as noted, the messages associated with identity are the only ones you can't override and alter.

code::
a = #(
	doesNotUnderstand: { |self, selector ...args, kwargs|
		"I'm sorry Dave, I'm afraid I can't do that."
	},
	'===': \meow,
	'!==': \meow,
	'identityHash': \meow  
);
a === a; // true, does NOT return \meow
a !== a; // true, does NOT return \meow
a.identityHash; // some number, does NOT return \meow
::



This also applies to inheritance chains.

code::
p = #(doesNotUnderstand: \meow);

b = #(super: p);

b.foo // \meow

// Adding a new definition into b (doesn't replace p's but will catch all uncaught methods)
b.doesNotUnderstand = \woof;
 
b.foo // \woof

// Remove b's implementation, revealing p's
b.doesNotUnderstand = nil;

// Back to p's
b.foo // \meow 
::

subsection:: value

code::
a = #(value: { \meow });
a.(); // \meow
::

subsection:: isException, throw, reportError
code::
a = #(
	isException: true, 
	reportError: { "fake error".postln }, 
	throw: {|self| thisThread.handleError(self) } 
);

try {
	1.postln;
	a.throw; 
	2.postln; // never gets here
}
::

subsection:: asString, postln, print to the post window
code::
a = #(asString: "meow", postln: "woof" ) // meow
a // meow
a.postln // woof
::

subsection:: class, isKindOf, pretending the object is not a proto object.
This could be very confusing, and isn't recommended unless absolutely necessary, but it is possible when needed.
code::
a = #(class: String, isKindOf: {|self, other| other == self.class });
a.class; // String
a.isKindOf(String); // true
a.isKindOf(Boolean); // false
::
